WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.802
-You know our next guest
from her work on

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.637
"Saturday Night Live,"
which airs this Saturday

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:07.573
on NBC and Peacock,
with host Brendan Gleeson.

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:11.344
Please welcome to the
show Sarah Sherman, everybody!

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:12.879
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:21.687 --> 00:00:23.790
I am so happy
you're here, Sarah.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.524
-I'm happy to be here, too.

00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:27.927
-So, you're back in New York
to start a second season.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:30.063
I know you did stand-up
over the summer.

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:33.065
Was it different doing stand-up
after a year on "SNL"?

00:00:33.065 --> 00:00:36.302
-Yeah, because, like,
people came to my stand-up shows

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:37.871
being like, "Oh, there's, like,
the girl with brown hair

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:39.204
from 'SNL.'

00:00:39.204 --> 00:00:42.441
I'm gonna see her do
tame, normal, polite stand-up."

00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:46.312
And they were not prepared for
very rude, scary stand-up.

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:47.747
-Yeah.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:48.982
-I'm, like, up there, like,

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:51.985
snorting like
a warthog, screaming.

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:54.988
-So we see the tamest version
of you on "SNL."

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:57.456
-I think so, yeah.

00:00:57.456 --> 00:01:00.159
-You have described
your style of comedy as

00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:01.494
"body horror comedy."

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:02.762
-Yeah.

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:05.531
-Which is a lovely genre
that I didn't even know existed.

00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:08.567
-Yeah, and it shouldn't.
It's very gross.

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:11.337
And it's disgusting,
and I should behave.

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:14.073
-But the interesting thing
about body horror comedy is,

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:16.842
it would never occur to me that
Lorne Michaels would think,

00:01:16.842 --> 00:01:18.110
"That's what we need
on the show."

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.446
Now, it's turned out to be
a really nice fit,

00:01:20.446 --> 00:01:22.515
but when you were
doing that style of comedy,

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:25.418
did you ever think to yourself,
"Oh, man, once 'SNL' sees this,

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:26.919
I'm gonna be right
in that cast"?

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:30.389
-I definitely did not think that
doing stand-up shows

00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:32.992
where I was, like,
screaming, like "Aaaah!"

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:36.195
and, like -- You know,
that's what it's like, the show.

00:01:36.195 --> 00:01:37.730
You guys are laughing.
You love it.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.632
And I would, like,
do stand-up shows where

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:42.969
I'm, like, screaming, and then
showing videos of, like,

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:47.373
a papier-mâché butthole
with, like,

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:49.275
Hamburger Helper meat
coming out of it.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.543
[ Audience groans ]

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:54.914
And, like -- Oh, you think
that's gross? Really?

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:58.184
Have you seen
the gas prices lately?

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:00.352
They're disgusting.
-You're very political.

00:02:00.352 --> 00:02:01.487
You've always been
very political.

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:03.356
-Absolutely, Seth.

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:05.124
But, like, you know,
doing shows like that,

00:02:05.124 --> 00:02:06.792
I never would have thought
that that would be

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:09.729
necessarily a
job application for "SNL."

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:13.533
-It has -- I think one of
the most unique things about

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.134
your first year is,
there were things that

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:17.437
you did on the show
that I don't think anyone

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:19.672
before would ever occur to them.

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:21.374
You did a wonderful sketch
called "Meatballs"

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:23.810
where you had a body
where you had singing meatballs.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.245
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:28.647
The wonderful Oscar Isaac played
one of the singing meatballs.

00:02:28.647 --> 00:02:31.083
This is a sense of --
I guess that's where --

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:32.485
Is that where Oscar's head was?

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:34.554
-Yeah, his little head
went in there.

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:37.289
-And he was on the show when
I was talking to him about it,

00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:39.292
and, you know, he gets
approached all the time

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:41.394
about "Meatballs," maybe more
than anything else he's done.

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:42.762
-Are you for real?

00:02:42.762 --> 00:02:45.764
-He said it's really been a nice
piece of feedback for him,

00:02:45.764 --> 00:02:48.334
and it doesn't really bump
against any of his other work,

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.469
his "Meatball" work.

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:51.570
-That's hilarious.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.807
"Oh, Oscar Isaac, you were great
in 'Star Wars' or whatever,

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:57.777
but loved you as a meatball."

00:02:57.777 --> 00:03:01.447
-You also had a wonderful sketch
where you played Chucky.

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:05.351
This is really
the impressive thing,

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:09.021
because this is --
They CGI'd your face onto this.

00:03:09.021 --> 00:03:11.056
But just to get a sense of
how many people have to work

00:03:11.056 --> 00:03:15.460
to get Chucky -- I mean,
that's a three-team puppeteer.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:16.629
-Can you believe this?

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.030
Like, a dumb idea like,

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:22.134
"Hey, what if my real head
was a on a puppet Chucky body?

00:03:22.134 --> 00:03:25.604
Can you guys, like, go above
and beyond to make this happen?"

00:03:25.604 --> 00:03:27.473
Like, literally
all these puppeteers --

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:30.510
Monkey Boys, shout-out.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.045
They're the name of
the puppeteers.

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:36.014
And, like, they all have to get
keyed out of this, like, scene,

00:03:36.014 --> 00:03:37.817
because they're, like,
there making the puppet move,

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:39.651
and they all have to get,
like, erased in the edit.

00:03:39.651 --> 00:03:40.886
Like, that's amazing.

00:03:40.886 --> 00:03:43.089
-I mean, it is the
greatest thing about "SNL,"

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:46.391
which is dumb ideas
are then executed by --

00:03:46.391 --> 00:03:49.228
But, no, genuinely, I --
Where just you have a dumb idea

00:03:49.228 --> 00:03:51.864
in the middle of the night,
and then professionals

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:53.098
have to execute it.

00:03:53.098 --> 00:03:55.401
And every time it happens,

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:57.269
you just feel
so incredibly grateful.

00:03:57.269 --> 00:03:58.338
-It's, like, amazing.

00:03:58.338 --> 00:04:00.106
I was like,
"I can't believe, like,

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:02.241
the best artists
in the world are, like,

00:04:02.241 --> 00:04:07.346
actively gluing a meatball
into my armpit

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:09.215
that is gonna eventually have

00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:12.051
Charli XCX's
singing face on it."

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:14.654
Like, it's just --
Like, it's amazing.

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:16.656
-You -- Because you are
someone who --

00:04:16.656 --> 00:04:18.725
Because of the style of the
show you were doing,

00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:20.826
you were doing
a ton of your own makeup.

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:22.895
And because
you were also doing videos,

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:24.864
a lot of special effects --

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:26.966
I mean, you explained
your papier-mâché butthole,

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:28.701
which we all, I think,
are huge fans of.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:29.868
-Sorry about that.

00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:31.504
-But this is a -- What --

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:32.838
So, you know what
I'm about to show you.

00:04:32.838 --> 00:04:34.507
This is a meat suit?

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:35.875
-Yeah, it's a meat suit.
-Okay.

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:39.578
-Yeah.
-I do want to prepare people.

00:04:39.578 --> 00:04:40.780
Yeah, you know what?
Maybe I'm not going to prepare.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:41.848
-Relax.
-Just take a look.

00:04:46.151 --> 00:04:47.820
I mean, it's real. Yeah.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.655
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:55.194
And this is -- this is --
I mean, this is D.I.Y.

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:56.729
This is all -- And that --

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:59.398
Now, that does look to me
like actual meat.

00:04:59.398 --> 00:05:01.467
-And, Seth,
I hate to break it to you,

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:03.636
but it truly is actual meat.

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:06.139
Well, 'cause I was, like --
When I was living in Chicago,

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:07.540
I'm like, "I have 30 bucks.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.076
How do I make it look like
I've got no skin?"

00:05:10.076 --> 00:05:12.144
-Yes.
-So, of course, what do I do?

00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:18.384
I superglued prosciutto
and bacon to my face.

00:05:18.384 --> 00:05:23.556
And then the teeth are just,
like, press-on nails.

00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.458
And the eyeballs are
Ping-Pong balls, of course.

00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:26.926
-Yeah.

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:28.394
-But I was like, "How do I make
it look like I'm covered --

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:30.763
like I'm just gore
and I have no skin?"

00:05:30.763 --> 00:05:33.199
So I was like, "I'll just glue a
bunch of raw meat to me."

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:35.301
And then, now that I, like,
work in, like,

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:37.736
professional, fancy,
adult settings,

00:05:37.736 --> 00:05:40.406
people are like,
"Yeah, you don't have to, like,

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:43.976
use real raw meat to do that.

00:05:43.976 --> 00:05:46.011
You can just absolutely
not do that."

00:05:46.011 --> 00:05:48.714
And that stank badly.

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:51.417
-I do -- I just do like that
you were living in Chicago

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:52.685
and the solution was meat.

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:55.555
Seems very, like, Chicago.
-Totally.

00:05:55.555 --> 00:05:57.189
-And everything in Chicago,

00:05:57.189 --> 00:06:01.160
a lot of solutions are just,
"Go get prosciutto."

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.529
You -- I'm wondering how --

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:05.597
You know, I think one of the
coolest things about "SNL" is,

00:06:05.597 --> 00:06:08.233
it's a show that your parents
know when you get the job.

00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:11.069
Are they happy about
this chapter in your life?

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:13.473
-It's like the best thing
that's ever happened to them.

00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:15.441
It's like my Bat Mitzvah
all over again.

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:18.144
Like, I think they got a little
worried when they saw, like,

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:22.715
the comedy that I was doing was
like, you know, crazy.

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:25.050
I'm popping huge pimples
on my face,

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.320
and, like, cottage cheese
is squirting out of them.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.389
Like, I think they were
a little nervous.

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:32.892
And then once, like,
I got on "SNL," they're like,

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:35.961
"I guess we're
not nervous anymore?"

00:06:35.961 --> 00:06:37.630
-Where were you
when you got the call?

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.598
-Well, actually, funny enough --
I swear to God --

00:06:39.598 --> 00:06:44.837
I was at home on Long Island,
with my parents and my grandma,

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:47.673
eating spaghetti and meatballs

00:06:47.673 --> 00:06:50.543
when I got the call
that I got "SNL."

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:52.177
And then, little did I know,

00:06:52.177 --> 00:06:55.847
meatballs was gonna factor
hugely into my life.

00:06:55.847 --> 00:06:57.083
-I thought -- I like the idea
that you got the call,

00:06:57.083 --> 00:06:58.251
and think, "Oh, my God.

00:06:58.251 --> 00:06:59.618
What am I gonna do for
my first sketch?"

00:06:59.618 --> 00:07:03.022
And then, as a meatball rose
towards your mouth...

00:07:03.022 --> 00:07:04.924
-Well, what's so great
about "SNL," is, like,

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:06.993
when I got the job,
I was like, "Oh, God,

00:07:06.993 --> 00:07:08.661
what am I going to do
on the show?"

00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:10.296
Like, "The only impression
I can do is, like,

00:07:10.296 --> 00:07:12.397
Stewie Griffin or something."

00:07:12.397 --> 00:07:15.034
From "Family Guy."
Hilarious little guy.

00:07:15.034 --> 00:07:17.069
Little baby, big head. Whatever.

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:18.370
I was like,
"What am I gonna do?"

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:21.173
And then, so, I was
a little nervous for a second,

00:07:21.173 --> 00:07:23.176
and then all the writers are,
like, so amazing.

00:07:23.176 --> 00:07:24.643
Like, there's this writer,
Dan Bulla,

00:07:24.643 --> 00:07:27.346
who called me, and he was like,
"I have an idea for you.

00:07:27.346 --> 00:07:29.081
You do, like,
body horror comedy.

00:07:29.081 --> 00:07:31.350
What if you, like,
were covered in meatballs?"

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.051
And I was like...
[ Gasps ]

00:07:33.051 --> 00:07:36.455
..."Everything's gonna be okay!

00:07:36.455 --> 00:07:38.491
Oh, my God! This is amazing!"

00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:39.925
-I would say
it's more than okay.

00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:41.560
And I'm so happy you're here.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:43.662
It has just been a delight to
watch you on "SNL."

00:07:43.662 --> 00:07:45.363
And I'm especially --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:45.363 --> 00:07:47.766
especially grateful to have you
here with us today.

00:07:47.766 --> 00:07:51.170
-Cool. Thanks for having me.
-You guys, that's Sarah Sherman.

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.839
"SNL" airs this Saturday
on NBC and Peacock

00:07:53.839 --> 00:07:56.475
with host Brendan Gleeson
and musical guest Willow.

00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:58.077
We'll be back with more
"Late Night."

